WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.253
Bonus distinction, power is
energy transferred per second.

00:00:01.662 --> 00:00:04.945
100 joules in five seconds is 20 watts.

00:00:05.456 --> 00:00:08.742
A higher power device does not
necessarily use more total energy

00:00:08.745 --> 00:00:13.550
if it operates for a shorter time
Energy depends on power and duration.

00:00:13.965 --> 00:00:16.679
Joules are the amount, watts are the rate

00:00:17.028 --> 00:00:22.558
Sort coal, natural gas, uranium,
sunlight and wind into renewable

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.446
and non-renewable resources.

00:00:25.245 --> 00:00:28.864
Coal and gas are fossil fuels
used much faster than they form.

00:00:29.332 --> 00:00:31.703
Uranium fuel is also non-renewable.

00:00:32.146 --> 00:00:35.358
Sunlight and wind are
replenished on human timescales.

00:00:35.987 --> 00:00:36.579
Correct.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.444
Renewable does not mean every local
resource is unlimited or impact-free.

00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:45.065
Biomass depends on sustainable
regrowth and land use.

00:00:45.426 --> 00:00:49.294
Burning it still releases carbon
dioxide and can create pollution.

00:00:50.094 --> 00:00:52.713
Low carbon and renewable
are different labels.

00:00:53.127 --> 00:00:57.520
Nuclear generation can be low carbon
over its life cycle while still using

00:00:57.615 --> 00:01:01.290
non-renewable fuel and requiring
radioactive waste management.

00:01:02.011 --> 00:01:02.816
Exactly.

00:01:03.071 --> 00:01:06.970
Fossil fuel combustion releases
carbon dioxide and other pollutants.

00:01:07.278 --> 00:01:09.752
Renewable technologies
also have construction,

00:01:09.923 --> 00:01:11.844
materials and habitat impacts.

00:01:12.025 --> 00:01:15.591
So compare whole systems rather
than claiming zero consequences.

00:01:16.341 --> 00:01:18.761
Solar output varies with
daylight and weather.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:20.868
Wind varies with conditions.

00:01:21.207 --> 00:01:25.421
Tides are predictable, while suitable
reservoir hydro can be more controllable.

00:01:25.897 --> 00:01:28.007
Different resources have
different strengths.

00:01:28.769 --> 00:01:29.155
Good.

00:01:29.534 --> 00:01:33.974
Storage, transmission links, demand
management, and a mix of generation

00:01:33.976 --> 00:01:35.891
can help balance supply and demand.

00:01:36.223 --> 00:01:40.873
Location, cost, construction time,
and environmental effects all matter.

00:01:41.703 --> 00:01:45.845
So an exam comparison should name a
specific advantage and disadvantage

00:01:45.849 --> 00:01:50.863
linked to the resource rather than simply
saying renewable good, non-renewable bad.

00:01:51.206 --> 00:01:54.422
Many power stations turn
turbines connected to generators.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.506
Does a generator create energy?

00:01:57.281 --> 00:01:57.581
No.

00:01:58.086 --> 00:02:01.876
It transfers energy from mechanical
motion into electrical output.

00:02:02.443 --> 00:02:05.616
Creating energy would bring back
the imaginary teacher tears.

00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:07.232
Exactly.

00:02:07.649 --> 00:02:11.686
Nuclear stations commonly use heat to
produce steam that drives turbines.

00:02:12.177 --> 00:02:16.698
Some fossil fuel stations also use
steam, while gas turbines can be driven

00:02:16.699 --> 00:02:18.859
directly by hot combustion gases.

00:02:19.925 --> 00:02:22.686
Combined cycle plants
use more than one stage.

00:02:23.502 --> 00:02:25.281
Wind turns blades directly.

00:02:25.754 --> 00:02:30.010
Hydroelectric systems use water moving
from a higher level to drive turbines.

00:02:30.444 --> 00:02:33.436
These routes are different, even
if a generator appears at the end.

00:02:34.212 --> 00:02:34.770
Correct.

00:02:35.083 --> 00:02:38.884
Photovoltaic cells convert
incoming radiation into electrical

00:02:38.893 --> 00:02:40.461
output without a turbine.

00:02:40.975 --> 00:02:42.991
A solar thermal system is different.

00:02:43.306 --> 00:02:47.147
It collects energy as heating,
sometimes for electricity generation

00:02:47.354 --> 00:02:49.393
and sometimes directly for hot water.

00:02:50.284 --> 00:02:53.369
Saving energy means reducing the
amount we need to supply for a

00:02:53.371 --> 00:02:55.911
purpose, not destroying less energy.

00:02:56.387 --> 00:03:01.253
Insulation, efficient appliances, and
switching off unneeded equipment can help.

00:03:01.979 --> 00:03:02.536
Yes.

00:03:02.884 --> 00:03:06.922
Compare the same useful service:
comfortable room temperature, adequate

00:03:06.922 --> 00:03:08.917
lighting, or a completed task.

00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:12.758
Saving energy can reduce costs
and resource use, though the

00:03:12.758 --> 00:03:16.215
environmental benefit depends on
the supply and the whole system.

00:03:16.994 --> 00:03:22.339
Final rescue: battery chemical store,
electrical transfer, light carried

00:03:22.343 --> 00:03:24.598
by radiation and unwanted heating.

00:03:25.137 --> 00:03:27.934
Lifting a book transfers
energy mechanically into the

00:03:27.935 --> 00:03:29.702
gravitational potential store.

00:03:30.018 --> 00:03:32.818
Energy conserved, useful outcome defined.

00:03:33.106 --> 00:03:35.166
Watts kept separate from joules?

00:03:35.973 --> 00:03:36.598
Perfect.

00:03:37.078 --> 00:03:40.832
Trace the stores, label the pathways,
and draw the system boundary.

00:03:41.217 --> 00:03:45.027
That is how an energy detective
solves the mystery without ever

00:03:45.028 --> 00:03:46.745
claiming that energy disappeared.
